/** \dir
This module offers classes and concepts for String and Char-operations, streaming,
conversion using Codec-classes and a StringList.
*/


namespace ptv {

/**
 * @brief The text-module which provides classes for String, Char, streaming and character coding using Codecs.
 */
namespace text {

/** \page "Tutorial - Usage of TextStream"
!!!Tutorial - Usage of TextStream

This tutorial will show how to use a TextStream object to read data from or write data to a stream while the character
data is automatically decoded or encoded.

!!Unicode character encoding

One of the most common standards for character encoding is the ASCII-standard. Each character is encoded using 7 bits of
a byte, so 128 different characters can be adressed. As those 128 characters are not enough to store most special characters
of every language available and most computers use 8 bits to store an information unit the 8th bit of the byte is also used
by the extended ASCII character table (8 bits - 256 different characters).

Reading and writing a character of the ASCII character table is straight-forward as each character is stored in exactly
one byte. No encoding or decoding is necesseray to write or read an ASCII text. All bytes can directly be read, stored to
memory and then used.

The draw-back of ASCII, of course, is the reduced character set of only 256 characters. There are a lot more characters than
that in languages all around the world.

This problem was addressed by the Unicode standard which was created to make every known character of the world available in
a single character table. Each character has a defined position in the table and so can be used as needed.

Unicode usually uses 16 bit to adress one character and so needs 2 bytes per character. This makes it possible to store
65536 different characters. Currently there are over 100.000 characters defined by the Unicode standard, so using 32 bits
becomes more common. This allows the storage of over 4 billion different characters. PPR uses a 32-bit value to store a single
character with the class ptv::text::Char. So a 32-bit Unicode character can be stored directly using PPR.

As most legacy systems can only store and handle 8-bit values there were several encodings introduced to store Unicode characters
using only 8 bits. One character of Unicode is encoded into a character sequence of 1 or more bytes that will also function
with older system. The most commonly known and used Unicode encoding format is UTF-8 which stores one 16-bit-Unicode character
in a byte sequence of 1 to 4 characters, depending on which character is encoded.

To avoid misfunction in legacy systems the first 128 bytes of ASCII have the same bit-sequence as the first 128 Unicode characters.
The first 128 characters in the Unicode table can be stored in UTF8 using only 1 byte without any encoding or change in the bit
order. One character in 7-bit-ASCII is the exact same as in UTF-8.

Besides Unicode encoding a lot more encoders/decoders are common, for example for Base64, BCD, ... encoding/decoding.


!!Codecs

A character which was encoded using UTF-8 (or any other available encoding) can usually not used directly; a decoding has first to
be performed to determine the original 32-bit value which describes the character's position in the Unicode table. The general
term for encoding and decoding modules is "codec" which is an akronym for #co#der and #dec#oder. A codec is used to convert
a signal or data stream from one format into another format or from one medium into another.

PPR internally uses a 32-bit value to store a single character while many libraries still use 8-bit values to store character
data. Thus encoding/decoding is needed to use these libraries. PPR offers a very flexible Codec handling mechanism. Basically
PPR provides text streaming classes (TextStream) which use the instance of one of several special Codec-classes to encode or
decode the character data. While reading from or writing to the stream the streaming class will automically decode or encode
the character. Reading or writing encoded text is completely transparent to the PPR user and needs no extra work.

Currently PPR provides codec classes for UTF8-, UTF16- and UTF32-encoding/decoding. The instance of one of those classes is simply
passed to the constructor of the text stream class of PPR. The following code demonstrates how this can be done:

\code
	std::stringstream ss;
	ptv::text::TextStream textStream(ss, new ptv::text::Utf8Codec());
\endcode
The text stream classes of PPR wrap another stream ($ss$), in this case a stringstream of the STL. Other streams like file-streams
are supported as well. This stream is passed to the TextStream-constructor along with the codec (Utf8) to use for encoding/decoding.
Reading a text will use this Codec to decode the read data. Writing will use this Codec for encoding.


!!Example 1 - Reading ASCII using the UTF8-Codec

This example demonstrates how to read 7-bit ASCII text. The Utf8Codec is used here although we only provide ASCII data. This is
correct as ASCII is compatible to UTF8 as mentioned above. This code is supposed to only demonstrate the general TextStream and
Codec usage in PPR. As we use an ASCII text the text will not be encoded/decoded as 7-bit ASCII und UTF8 is compatible.

\code
std::stringstream ss;
ss << "abcdef";

ptv::text::TextStream textStream(ss, new ptv::text::Utf8Codec());

ptv::text::String s;
getline(textStream, s);

cout << s.str() << endl;
\endcode

First a stringstream is created and initialized with the string "abcdef". A PPR TextStream object is created for the stringstream
using the UTF8-codec automatically. One line is read from this stream using the function getline(). While reading the line the data
from the stringstream is decoded by the UTF8-codec. As the stringstream contains ASCII-data this will have do no real conversion.

This code produces this output, which is the same text as was written into to textstream at the first 2 lines of the program:
\code
abcdef
\endcode


!!Example 2 - Reading UTF8 using the UTF8-Codec

The usage of the TextStream class in combination with the Utf8Codec is more useful when reading real UTF8-data. The following
example demonstrates this usage:

\code
char utf8Data[] = { (char)0xce, (char)0xba, (char)0xe1, (char)0xbd, (char)0xb9, (char)0xcf, (char)0x83,
                    (char)0xce, (char)0xbc, (char)0xce, (char)0xb5, (char)0x0 };

std::stringstream ss(utf8Data);

ptv::text::TextStream textStream(ss, new ptv::text::Utf8Codec());

ptv::text::String s;
getline(textStream, s);
\endcode
The first two lines of code create a char-array which contains a UTF8-encoded word (5 characters) using some greek characters.
The Unicode character values before encoding are as follows: 954, 8057, 963, 956, 949, 0. These characters are stored in the
stringstream $ss$. In a real world program this stream may more likely be a file input stream and the data may for example be
a XML-file with UTF-8-encoding.

In the 3rd line a TextStream object is created for the stringstream using a UTF8-codec. The following lines of code read one
line from the TextStream and store the read data in a ptv::text::String which can handle 32-bit Unicode characters directly.
While reading the UTF8 data it is decoded to Unicode data and stored into the String object.

When printing the String $s$ to a device that supports the output of Unicode characters the following text will be visible:

| \image ppr-text-tutorial1-Unicode_String.png


!!Example 3 - Writing UTF8 using the UTF8-Codec

Writing a Unicode String using UTF8-encoding is as simple as reading UTF8-data as demonstrates in example 2. Basically a TextStream
object is created to which the wrapped output stream and the Codec object is passed. The following code demonstrates this behaviour:

\code
ptv::text::Char unicodeData[] = { 954, 8057, 963, 956, 949, 0 };

std::stringstream ss;
ptv::text::TextStream textStream(ss, new ptv::text::Utf8Codec());

textStream << unicodeData;
\endcode
The Unicode character data to convert to UTF8 is written into a ptv::text::Char-array in the first line. This Char class
uses 32-bit values to store Unicode characters and can thus store the Unicode characters directly without encoding. The
Unicode-text is the same as used in example 2.

A stringstream object is created which will function as wrapped stream to which the UTF8-encoded characters (byte) are
written to. The stringstream is passed to the TextStream and the Utf8Codec is assigned to the TextStream for encoding.
Writing Unicode data to this TextStream object will encode the data into UTF8 and write this data into $ss$.

The Unicode data is written to the TextStream object in the last line. By doing this the Unicode data is converted to
UTF8 and written to the stringstream $ss$. Reading the data in $ss$ will return the same character sequence as seen in the
first line of the example code of example 2.

*/


}
}